=== night_3 ===

= costume_room_3

The costume room is quieter tonight. Fewer actors. The energy is different: loose, unhurried. Someone checks their phone openly. The woman with the ribcage piece isn't here.

"Slow night," the guy at the next station says to nobody in particular. "You can always tell by the parking lot."

Bex is at their station.

{visited_restricted_passage:
    {post_shift_2_tell_bex: They work fast. Prosthetic gash, greasepaint, mask. Efficient hands, no eye contact. The "please" from last night sits between you like something neither of you wants to pick up.}
    {post_shift_2_keep_quiet: They work at normal pace. Professional. But once, when they hand you the mask, their eyes drop to your hands. The copper stains are gone. Bex remembers them anyway.}
    {not post_shift_2_tell_bex && not post_shift_2_keep_quiet: They work at their usual pace. Prosthetic gash, greasepaint, mask. You feel the weight of what you found last night, but Bex's expression gives nothing away. Same routine.}
}
{not visited_restricted_passage: They work at their usual pace. Prosthetic gash, greasepaint, mask. Same routine, third night running.}

"New zone again. The Buried."

* "What's The Buried?"
    "Tightest zone in the uppers. Cave-in aftermath. Low ceilings, rubble walls, squeeze-throughs. Guests hate it." A beat. "That's the point."
    -> buried_briefing
* "Dale's rotating me again?"
    "Standard for new hires. Three zones in three nights. He wants to see where you fit." Bex pauses. "Or where to put you."
    -> buried_briefing
* "How's the crowd tonight?"
    "Light tonight. You'll get maybe twenty groups all night." Bex shrugs. "Slow nights are their own thing. More time in the dark."
    -> buried_briefing

= buried_briefing

"Your position is a rubble gap. There's a slit in the debris wall, about shoulder-width, stone behind you and foam in front. You wedge yourself in sideways and wait. When a group passes, you push through the rubble into the corridor."

Bex demonstrates with their hands, one flat surface pressing through another.

"Arms first. Then shoulders. The foam shifts when you come through. Pieces fall. The guests hear the wall moving before they see you. On a slow night like this, take it deliberate. Let them watch the rubble shift. Let them figure out what's happening. By the time you're through, they've had three seconds of knowing something is coming out of the wall and not being able to do anything about it."

* "More dread than startle."
    "Exactly. Slow nights, you lean into the dread. The gaps between groups are long. Use the silence. The zone does half the work for you. The ceilings are low enough that everyone's already uncomfortable before you do anything."
    -> buried_briefing_2
* "What if I get stuck?"
    "You won't. The gap is tight but the foam gives. Just don't overthink it. Push and commit." They almost smile. "If you can breathe, you can fit."
    -> buried_briefing_2

= buried_briefing_2

"Three other actors in The Buried tonight. Adjacent positions, same corridor. You'll be able to hear each other between groups. That's normal. Keep the chatter quiet."

Bex hands you the soft-soled boots again.

"Same rules as always. Don't leave your zone. Don't break character in front of guests." They hesitate, then add: "The Buried can feel... close. More than the other zones. The ceilings, the walls. If you need to step out for air, tell your floor manager. Nobody will judge you."

It sounds like standard advice. It also sounds like it isn't.

-> run_through_3

= run_through_3

6:45 PM. Pre-show briefing. Thinner crowd than the past two nights. Dale does the speech. Same words. Same clipboard.

"Light house tonight. Stay sharp anyway. Make it real, people."

The actors disperse. Fewer of them tonight. You count maybe twenty-five, down from thirty-five on Night 1. The zones will be spread thin.

You follow the markers to Zone 3: The Buried.

-> enter_buried

= enter_buried

The Buried earns its name.

The transition from the main corridor is immediate. The ceiling drops. Not gradually. One step you're in a standard tunnel, the next you're ducking. The clearance is maybe five and a half feet, and the set dressing makes it feel lower: cracked timber supports bowed under the weight of foam rock, dangling cables (fake, but convincing), a fine dust haze in the air that catches the dim amber light.

The corridor is narrow. Two people can't walk abreast. The walls are dressed as collapsed rubble: foam boulders, splintered wood, twisted metal brackets. But behind the dressing, you can feel the real stone. The mine walls are right there, inches behind the set.

Your position is about forty feet in. The rubble gap.

It's exactly what Bex described. A vertical slit in the debris wall, maybe eighteen inches wide. Behind it, a pocket of space between the foam dressing and the real stone wall. You turn sideways and squeeze in.

Stone against your back. Cool, rough, real. Foam rubble against your chest and face. Through a gap in the debris, you can see the corridor: a narrow strip of amber light, fog pooling on the ground, the low ceiling pressing down.

The space is tight enough that your breathing moves the foam in front of you. You can feel your ribs expand against the stone on each inhale.

You test the emergence. Push forward with your hips and shoulders. The foam shifts, pieces tumbling. Your arms come through first, fingers scraping the corridor floor, then your shoulders, then the rest of you, spilling out of the wall in a cascade of debris.

You pull yourself back in. Reset the foam pieces. Wedge back into the gap.

To your left, maybe fifteen feet away, you can hear someone else settling into their position. A cough. The scrape of boots on stone.

Radio crackle. "Doors open in five. Positions."

You press into the gap. You wait.

-> first_buried_groups

= first_buried_groups

The first group enters The Buried at 8:11 PM.

You hear them before you see them. The sounds are different in here: muffled, close, compressed by the low ceiling. Footsteps on gravel. Someone saying "oh my God, it's so small in here." Nervous laughter.

They pass your position. Through the gap in the foam, you see them: four people, hunched, one hand on the ceiling, moving slowly. They're already scared. The zone is doing the work.

You push.

Arms first. The foam shifts. A chunk of rubble (lightweight, painted gray) tumbles into the corridor. The person at the back of the group hears it and turns.

You keep pushing. Shoulders through. The debris cascades. You're emerging from the wall, slow, deliberate, a dead miner pulling itself free from the collapse that killed it.

The person at the back stares. One second. Two. Processing. Then the scream.

The group doesn't run. There's nowhere to run. The corridor is too tight. They compress forward, stumbling, ducking, hands on each other's backs. You hold the position: half out of the wall, arms extended, mask facing them. They scramble around the corner and are gone.

You pull back in. Reset the foam. Wedge into the gap.

Silence. The dust settles. The amber light is steady.

* [Wait for the next group.]
    -> slow_night_rhythm

= slow_night_rhythm

The next group doesn't come for four minutes.

Four minutes in the gap. Stone behind you. Foam in front. The mask fogs with your breath. Your shoulders ache from the narrow space. Dust from the foam settles on your tongue whenever you inhale through your mouth.

This is the slow night. The full houses on Nights 1 and 2 were machines: group after group, scream after scream, no time to think. Tonight there's nothing but time. The mine fills the gaps. You hear the timber creak. Water dripping somewhere below. And the hum, always the hum, louder in the silence than it ever was under the noise of a full crowd.

You start to find the pace. When a group comes, you emerge slow. Let them see the wall move. Let the foam tumble. Let the dread build. On a full night you'd need speed. Tonight you have the luxury of patience, and patience is worse.

Between groups, you stand in the dark and listen to the mine breathe.

By 9:30, you've done maybe ten groups. On a full night you'd be at thirty by now.

The actor in the next position over is bored. You can hear them shifting, tapping a rhythm on the stone with their fingernails. During a long lull, a whisper comes through the wall.

"Hey. New hire, right?"

* "Yeah. Third night."
    "Third night on a dead one. Lucky you." A quiet laugh. "I'm Tuck."
    -> tuck_banter_hub
* "Val."
    "Tuck." A pause. "Slow one tonight. You holding up?"
    -> tuck_banter_hub

= tuck_banter_hub

Tuck is two positions down, close enough to whisper between groups. You can't see each other. Just voices through the rubble wall.

* {not tuck_dale} [Ask about Dale.]
    -> tuck_dale
* {not tuck_bex} [Ask about Bex.]
    -> tuck_bex
* {not tuck_staying} [Ask how long they've been here.]
    -> tuck_staying
* {tuck_dale || tuck_bex || tuck_staying} [A group is coming. Back to work.]
    -> buried_mid_shift

= tuck_dale

"What's the deal with Dale?"

A pause. You hear Tuck shift in their position.

"Dale's Dale. Always smiling. Always has the clipboard. He's fine." Another pause. "I've been here... I think about eight months? Maybe longer. And he's never had a bad day. Like, never. Not one."

* "That's a little weird."
    "I guess? Some people are just like that. Positive energy or whatever." You can hear the shrug in their voice. "He's not a bad boss. Pays on time. Doesn't micromanage. Just... don't be late and don't wander."
    {dale_suspicion >= 5: Then, quieter: "He's been asking about you, though. Just heads up."}
    -> tuck_banter_hub
* "Does he ever talk about the company? HorrorCo?"
    "Not really. You get the briefing, you get the schedule, you get the 'make it real' speech. That's about it. Corporate is..." Tuck trails off. "I don't think anyone's actually met anyone from corporate. Dale's the whole chain of command."
    {dale_suspicion >= 5: Then, quieter: "He's been asking about you, though. Just so you know."}
    -> tuck_banter_hub

= tuck_bex

"Do you know Bex?"

"Bex is Bex." Tuck says it the same way everyone says it. Like a fact of geography. "They've been here longer than anyone. Someone told me they were here when the place opened, but that can't be right. They'd have to be... I don't know. They don't look old enough."

* "Do they ever talk about how long they've been here?"
    "I asked once. They got this look. Not angry. More like..." Tuck pauses. "Like I'd asked them what year it was and they weren't sure of the answer. I didn't ask again."
    -> tuck_banter_hub
* "Are they always that quiet?"
    "Pretty much. They do their job. They help new hires. But they're kind of... apart. Like they're on a different frequency than everyone else."
    -> tuck_banter_hub

= tuck_staying

"How long have you been at The Hollows?"

"Eight months. I think. Maybe nine." A beat. "Came out from Reno for a couple weeks. Just needed the money. Then I stayed."

* "You like it that much?"
    "I don't know if 'like' is the word. It's just... there's always another shift. And the town is easy. Cheap motel. The diner's fine. You don't really need much out here."
    -> tuck_leaving
* "Ever think about leaving?"
    -> tuck_leaving

= tuck_leaving

"Leaving?" Tuck repeats the word slowly. "I don't know. I guess I haven't really thought about it. Huh."

They say this without concern. Without any weight at all. The way you'd say you hadn't thought about what to have for breakfast. Not a decision. Just an absence where a decision should be.

"There's always another shift," they say again. Like a refrain they don't know they're repeating.

-> tuck_banter_hub

= buried_mid_shift

Groups keep coming. Slowly. You push through the wall, they scream, you reset. The rhythm is sparse and steady. Your core aches from the repeated push-and-wedge. The mask has rubbed a second raw line across your forehead, parallel to the first.

Between scares, you listen. The mine. The hum. Tuck humming tunelessly in the next gap over. The other two actors are further down and you only hear them as distant thumps and muffled shouts.

Around 10:30, you notice something. The gap between your last two groups was seven minutes. Before that, five. The crowd is thinning. The show is winding down early tonight.

Then the radio.

"Val Rowan, Zone 3. Report to the main corridor. Manager request."

You push out of the gap. Stretch your shoulders. Walk to the corridor intersection where the amber light meets the fluorescent white of the service hallway.

Dale is waiting.

{dale_suspicion >= 5: -> dale_perimeter}
{dale_suspicion < 5: -> dale_zone_5}

= dale_perimeter

His smile is thinner tonight. Not gone. Just thinner.

"Change of plans, Val. I'm pulling you off the floor. Need you on perimeter."

Perimeter. Parking lot duty. Crowd control outside the building.

"Take a radio. Direct traffic. Make sure nobody parks in the fire lanes."

* "On a slow night?"
    "Every night, full or slow. Insurance requirement." He holds your gaze. "You can handle it."
    -> perimeter_assignment
* "Understood."
    -> perimeter_assignment
* "Is there a reason I'm being pulled?"
    ~ dale_suspicion += 1
    "Overstaffed for the house size. Happens on slow nights." His smile doesn't change. "Don't read into it."
    -> perimeter_assignment

= perimeter_assignment

He hands you a reflective vest. Neon yellow, cheap, the kind road crews wear. Over your dead miner costume, it looks absurd.

"Leave the mask. Keep the radio on channel 1."

He turns and walks back into the tunnels. His footsteps are unhurried. He doesn't look back.

You walk to the staff exit. Push through the door. Outside.

-> perimeter_shift

= perimeter_shift

The parking lot is less than a third full. Maybe twenty cars, scattered across a lot built for two hundred. The ticket booth is dark. The queue barriers stand empty, casting long shadows under the pole lights.

It's cold. Desert cold, the kind that drops fast after sunset. The reflective vest does nothing for warmth. You stand at the edge of the lot, radio in hand, directing nobody.

A car pulls in. Parks. A couple gets out, walks to the entrance. They don't look at you.

Silence.

The building sits against the mountain, a dark mass of concrete and stone. You can hear the muffled bass of the attraction's audio through the walls. Somewhere inside, thirty actors are scaring the last few dozen guests of the night. You're out here watching an empty parking lot.

{dale_suspicion >= 6: Dale didn't pull you for staffing reasons. He pulled you because you're asking questions.{employee_records_seen: Because you stayed late at the terminal.}{library_clerk_check: Because the library clerk reported back.} Because you pushed. This is the parking lot version of a corner office with no phone.}

You walk the perimeter. Slowly. Observing.

The front of the building is all show: the arch, the lights (half of them off tonight), the fog machines (silent). The loading dock on the east side is padlocked. Ventilation ducts hum on the roof.

You circle around to the back.

-> perimeter_discovery

= perimeter_discovery

The back of the building faces the mountain. No lights. No signage. Just raw concrete meeting raw stone where the structure transitions into the mine itself.

Three emergency exits, evenly spaced. Steel doors, crash bars, marked with small red signs. The first two are shut. The third is propped open with a rock. Just a few inches. Enough for a thin line of warm air to spill out into the cold.

The hum is louder here. Not the muffled version from the parking lot. Clear and present, like standing next to a transformer.

You put your hand near the gap. The air is warm, almost hot. It smells like copper and ozone.

* [Go through.]
    You glance back toward the parking lot. Empty. Nobody watching. The radio crackles with routine chatter from inside.
    You pull the door open enough to slip through. The warm air wraps around you.
    -> service_corridor
* [Note the location. Stay outside.]
    You memorize the position. Third exit from the east side, propped open, warm air. You can come back. But not tonight. Not in a reflective vest with a radio tuned to Dale's channel.
    -> perimeter_late
* [Close the door.]
    You kick the rock out and let the door shut. The crash bar engages with a heavy click. The warm air cuts off. The hum fades.
    Whatever was supposed to be going in or out of that door tonight, it isn't anymore.
    -> perimeter_late

= service_corridor

~ erasure_level += 1

A service corridor. Not part of the attraction. No set dressing, no speakers, no fog. Just poured concrete floor, utility lighting, exposed pipes along the ceiling. A fire extinguisher on the wall. An electrical panel. The bones of the building, stripped of performance.

The corridor runs deeper into the mountain. The concrete floor gives way to stone after about thirty feet. The utility lights continue but they're spaced further apart, pools of yellow in growing darkness.

You're behind the show. This is the space between the walls, the infrastructure that guests never see and actors aren't supposed to either. Service access for the mid-level.

{has_mara_pages: Mara's map showed the service corridors. She marked them in blue. You're standing on a blue line right now.}

The walls transition. Concrete to stone. New construction to old mine. You can see the seam where the building ends and the earth begins. Someone has bolted a steel bracket to the stone with a sign: AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY BEYOND THIS POINT.

Past the sign, the corridor opens slightly. The ceiling is higher here, natural rock instead of poured concrete. Two passages branch off: one left, one right. Both dark. The hum comes from the left passage, steady and low.

The right passage has a light at the far end. A single bulb, caged, the kind used in maintenance areas. Under it, on the stone wall, you can see marks. Scratches. No. Carvings.

Symbols.

{has_symbol_knowledge: The same angular, geometric patterns from the maintenance tunnel. Mara's notebook page. But these are larger. Deeper. Cut into the stone with something heavy. And there are more of them, covering a wider area.}

{has_portal_mechanics: Containment sigils. That's what Silas said the Order's 1971 agent called them. These are part of the same system. The lock that was supposed to keep the portal shut.}

{not has_symbol_knowledge && not has_portal_mechanics: You don't recognize them. But they're deliberate. Someone carved these with purpose. They cover a section of wall maybe six feet wide, arranged in concentric arcs, like a target. Or a diagram. Or a map of something that spirals downward.}

You step closer. The carvings are old. The stone around them is smooth, worn. But at the bottom of the arrangement, newer scratches. Sharper. Fresh stone showing white against the dark surface.

Someone has been adding to them. Recently.

Your radio crackles. Loud in the silence.

"All perimeter, status check."

You key the radio. "Perimeter clear." Your voice sounds wrong in here. Flat. Absorbed by the stone.

* [Keep exploring.]
    ~ dale_suspicion += 1
    You move down the right passage toward the symbols. The hum from the left passage vibrates through the floor. The air is warm. Your skin prickles.
    The carvings extend further than you thought. They continue around a corner, down a short flight of rough-cut steps, and stop at a steel door. Modern. Heavy. A keycard reader blinks red on the wall beside it.
    The door to mid-level. One of them, anyway.
    You don't have a keycard. But you know it's here. You know the path.
    -> leave_service
* [Head back. You've seen enough.]
    You've pushed your luck. The radio check means someone is tracking positions. If you don't appear at the parking lot soon, questions get asked.
    You turn and walk back through the service corridor, through the propped exit, into the cold night air. The warm air cuts off behind you like a held breath releasing.
    -> perimeter_late

= leave_service

You retrace your steps. Service corridor. Concrete floor. The propped exit. Cold air.

You pull the door almost closed, leaving the rock in place. Whatever reason it was propped open, you don't want to be the one who changed things.

-> perimeter_late

= dale_zone_5

His smile is full. Operational, not personal.

"Light night. I'm consolidating zones. Need you to cover second half in Zone 5."

Zone 5. You haven't worked it. Deeper in the uppers, past The Shaft, past The Collapse.

"Simple step-out. Behind the support beam at position C. You'll see the mark." He checks his clipboard. "Should be quiet the rest of the night. We're winding down early."

* "Got it."
    -> zone_5_transition
* "Where is Zone 5 exactly?"
    "Follow the main corridor past The Shaft. Take the second left. You'll see the signage." He's already turning away, already on to the next task. "Radio if you need anything."
    -> zone_5_transition

= zone_5_transition

You walk. Past The Shaft, where you worked last night. The blue-white wash is dimmer tonight, the fog thinner. The skeleton crew shows: gaps between scare positions, empty alcoves, zones running on half power.

Zone 5 is called THE DRIFT. The set dressing suggests a different section of the mine: rougher walls, an ore cart track running along the floor, the sound of pickaxes echoing from hidden speakers. The ceiling is normal height here. After The Buried, it feels cavernous.

Your position is behind a timber support beam at a bend in the corridor. Simple mechanics: wait for the group to pass, step out behind them, stand still until the last person notices you. The stalker walk from Night 2, but stationary.

Easy work. Especially on a slow night.

But that's not what holds your attention.

At the far end of Zone 5, past the final scare position, the corridor ends at a door. Not a set piece. A real door. Steel frame, simple latch, no keycard reader. A sign reads: STAFF ONLY - LOWER LEVEL ACCESS.

The stairwell to mid-level.

From your position, you can see it during every lull between groups. The door. The latch. The thin line of warm air seeping from underneath it.

-> zone_5_shift

= zone_5_shift

You work the zone. Groups come through, sparse and scattered. The step-out is easy after three nights of boo holes and stalker walks and squeeze-throughs. You stand behind the beam. They pass. You step out. They notice. They scream or they don't. You step back.

Between groups, you watch the door.

The warm air from underneath it pulses. Not steadily. Rhythmically. Like breathing. In. Out. The gap between the door and the floor glows faintly on the exhale, warm air catching the amber light.

A group passes. You do the scare. Reset.

The door.

Another group. Scare. Reset.

The door. Five minutes without a group. The radio is quiet. The show is winding down.

* [Try the door.]
    You walk to the end of the corridor. Fifteen steps past your position. You check the radio: silent. You check the corridor behind you: empty.
    The latch is warm. You push it.
    -> stairwell_descent
* [Stay at your position. Note the door for later.]
    You know where it is. Zone 5, end of the corridor. Simple latch. You can come back. During the day, maybe. Or tomorrow night, if Dale puts you back here.
    The warm air pulses under the door. In. Out.
    You watch it until the next group comes.
    -> zone_5_late
* [Listen at the door.]
    You walk to the door and press your ear against the steel.
    The hum. Louder than anywhere in the upper zones. And underneath it, sounds. Footsteps, maybe. Or water. Or machinery. Something moving below you, steady and purposeful.
    Then a sound you recognize: a door opening and closing, somewhere down the stairwell. Someone is down there.
    You step back to your position before the next group arrives.
    -> zone_5_late

= stairwell_descent

~ erasure_level += 1

The door opens onto a concrete stairwell. Utility lighting. The air is noticeably warmer. The hum is immediate, present, vibrating through the handrail when you grip it.

Down. One flight. A landing. Another flight.

The stairwell opens onto a corridor that is wider than anything in the upper zones. The ceiling is high. The walls are stone, not set-dressed. No plywood, no paint. Show lighting bleeds in from somewhere to the left: red and purple washes, fog drifting. To the right, the corridor is dark. Unlit. Raw mine tunnel continuing into the mountain.

This is mid-level. You're standing at the boundary between the attraction and the mine.

{has_mara_pages: Mara's map. You can picture it. The stairwell connects to the junction between Zones 7 and 8. The mid-level scare zones are to the left. The path to the deep rooms is to the right. Down.}

You can hear the show from here. Muffled screams. A bass hit from a speaker. The mechanical clank of an animatronic. The attraction is running, even on a slow night, even half-staffed, the machine keeps going.

To the right: darkness. The hum. The pull.

Your radio crackles.

"All zones, fifteen minutes to close. Last groups coming through."

* [Go back up. You know the way now.]
    You climb the stairs. Close the door behind you. Walk back to position C. Your heart is pounding. Your hands are warm from the handrail.
    A group comes through. You step out from behind the beam. They scream. You reset.
    The door is right there. You know what's behind it. You know the way down.
    -> zone_5_late
* [Take one more look to the right.]
    ~ dale_suspicion += 1
    You step into the dark corridor. The hum wraps around you. The air smells like copper. Ten steps. Twenty.
    The walls narrow. The ceiling drops. You're leaving mid-level and entering something older. The stone is different here: darker, smoother, like it's been polished by water or time or something else.
    Then footsteps. Above you. In the stairwell. Someone coming down.
    You press flat against the wall. A flashlight beam sweeps the junction. A floor manager, doing rounds.
    They don't see you. The beam passes. Footsteps continue down the left corridor, toward the scare zones.
    You wait thirty seconds, then move. Back to the junction. Up the stairs. Through the door. Back to position C.
    -> zone_5_late

= perimeter_late

The rest of the perimeter shift is cold and empty. Two more cars arrive. One leaves. The radio chatters with floor manager updates that have nothing to do with you.

At 1 AM, the last guests exit. The parking lot empties. The lights over the entrance shut off one by one.

{not service_corridor: You circle the building one more time. The third emergency exit is still propped open. The warm air still spills out. You leave it.}

At 1:30, the radio crackles: "Perimeter, you're clear. Come on in."

-> post_shift_3

= zone_5_late

The show closes early tonight. 1:30 AM instead of 2. The last group comes through at 1:15, a couple who barely react. Too tired or too drunk to be scared.

House lights up. Fog off. The ambient audio dies. Zone 5, in full light, is just a corridor with a beam in it.

The door at the far end is still there. The warm air still pulses underneath it. In the fluorescent light, you can see scuff marks on the floor in front of it. Someone uses this door regularly.

-> post_shift_3

= post_shift_3

Break room.

Tuck is there, changed out of costume, eating chips from a vending machine bag. They wave. "Survive the night?"

"Barely."

Tuck laughs. Easy, unbothered. Same person who said "I guess I haven't thought about it" when asked about leaving. They seem fine. They seem normal. That's the thing.

{dale_suspicion >= 5:
    Bex appears. They look at you, then at the reflective vest you're still wearing.
    "Dale pulled you outside?"
    Their voice is flat. But their eyes say something else.
    * "Yeah. Perimeter duty."
        Bex nods slowly. "He did that to someone else. A while ago." They pause. "Dark hair. She had dark hair. And she..." Bex closes their eyes. Opens them. "I can't remember her name. I should remember her name."
        {mara_clues >= 2: Dark hair. Sharp eyes. The woman Gloria described, the one who sat at the counter and ordered black coffee and meatloaf. Mara. Bex is describing Mara, and the name is dissolving out of them even as they reach for it.}
        {mara_clues == 1: Dark hair. The description catches on something. Someone else who worked here. Someone else who asked questions.}
        -> post_shift_3_bex
    * "Is that bad?"
        "It's not good." Bex sits down. "It means he's decided something about you."
        -> post_shift_3_bex
    * [Say nothing.]
        Bex looks at you for a long moment. Then they turn and start removing their makeup.
        -> post_shift_3_end
}

{dale_suspicion < 5:
    Bex is at their station. Quiet. Routine.
    {stairwell_descent:
        Your hands are warm. You can still feel the hum in your fingertips from the handrail. You found the way down. Mid-level. The junction. The path to the deep rooms.
        Bex glances at you once. Their eyes linger on your hands, still flushed from the warmth below.
        -> post_shift_3_bex
    }
    {not stairwell_descent:
        -> post_shift_3_end
    }
}

= post_shift_3_bex

* [Tell Bex what you saw.]
    ~ bex_relationship += 1
    {service_corridor: "I found a service corridor. Behind the building. It connects to mid-level. There are symbols carved into the walls. The same kind I found in the maintenance tunnel."}
    {stairwell_descent: "I found the stairwell to mid-level. Zone 5. I went down. I saw the junction."}
    {not service_corridor && not stairwell_descent: "I walked the perimeter. There's an emergency exit propped open on the back side. Warm air coming out.{bex_hum: The hum is stronger there."}}
    Bex listens. Their expression doesn't change.
    "You're going deeper."
    It's not a question.
    "Be careful, Val. The deeper you go, the harder it is to come back. And I don't mean physically."
    -> post_shift_3_end
* [Keep it to yourself.]
    ~ bex_relationship -= 1
    You shrug. "Just a slow night."
    Bex watches you for a moment. They know you're not telling them something. You can see it in the way they hold still, the way their eyes track your face.
    They don't push.
    -> post_shift_3_end

= post_shift_3_end

-> figure_absence

= figure_absence

{dale_suspicion >= 5:
    You realize it as you're walking out. The building. The parking lot. The corridors in the first half of the shift, the long cold silence of the perimeter in the second.
}
{dale_suspicion < 5:
    You realize it as you're walking out. The corridor. The zones. The gaps between groups, the long slow silences.
}

No figure.

Night 1, the Figure appeared in The Collapse. Night 2, in The Shaft, closer. Tonight: nothing. No motionless shape at the end of a corridor. No burlap mask. No faded silhouette standing in the dark.

You expected it. You were watching for it. Every long lull, every empty stretch, you found yourself scanning the shadows for something that shouldn't be there.

It wasn't.

{mara_clues >= 3: You wonder where Mara is tonight. If the Figure is what's left of her, if it's some kind of echo or residue, then its absence means something. She's not here. She's somewhere else. Deeper, maybe. Or further gone.}

{mara_clues < 3: The corridors were just corridors tonight. The dark was just dark. You should be relieved. You're not.}

-> end_night_3

= end_night_3

The walk back to the motel. Stars. Cold. The hum.

Room 6. The room accepted your existence today. Your bag is where you left it after re-unpacking this morning. Small comfort. You wonder if it will remember you tomorrow.

{has_mara_recording:
You take out the recorder. Press play. Mara's voice, low and steady.

"The attraction isn't just built on top of something. It's built to feed something. The fear. The guests' fear. It's not a byproduct. It's the product."

On a slow night, with a thin crowd and half the zones running on skeleton crew, the machine still ran. Still harvested. Whatever is below this mine, it ate less tonight. But it still ate.
}

{service_corridor: The symbols in the service corridor. {visited_restricted_passage: Larger than the ones in the maintenance tunnel. Older.} And someone is adding to them.}

{stairwell_descent: Mid-level. The junction. The path to the right, into the dark, toward the deep rooms. You know the way now. Tomorrow night, you can go further.}

{not service_corridor && not stairwell_descent: You didn't go deeper tonight. You stayed where you were told. But you know where the doors are. Tomorrow night.}

You set your alarm for noon.

